    "alternatives": [],
    "apply": "rebase-config",
    "caveats": [
      "Policy "posts_select_841c287" is compiled by Rebase from a collection's \`securityRules\` and dropped and re-created on every boot, so an ALTER POLICY would be undone at the next restart. Change the rule, then redeploy or run \`rebase db push\`. A collection that declares no \`securityRules\` of its own inherits \`defaultSecurityRules\` from config/collections/index.ts — a stock scaffold's unfiltered read rule lives there.",
    ],
    "placeholders": [],
    "rebase": {
      "applicable": true,
      "note": "Rebase enables row-level security on every collection's table and compiles each rule into a policy at boot and on \`rebase db push\`; \`ownerField\` compiles to \`<column>::text = rebase.uid()\`, so a row is visible and writable only to the user whose id it holds. https://rebase.pro/docs/collections/security-rules",
      "securityRules": "[
    { operation: "select", ownerField: "author_id" }
]",
      "snippet": "// The collection whose table is public.posts (config/collections/ in a Rebase project)
defineCollection({
    // …name, slug, properties and the rest stay as they are…
    table: "posts",
    securityRules: [
        { operation: "select", ownerField: "author_id" }
    ]
});",
    },
    "sql": null,
    "summary": "Replace the rule that compiles to policy "posts_select_841c287" on public.posts — an \`access: "public"\` rule, or a raw \`using: "true"\` — with one scoped to the row's owner.",
  },
}
`;

